WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:06.150
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:36.899 --> 00:00:43.460
This work is about creating facial animation from
speech in real time.

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:48.420
This means that after recording the audio
footage of us speaking, we give it to a learning

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:54.079
algorithm, which creates a high-quality animation
depicting our digital characters uttering

00:00:54.079 --> 00:00:55.350
these words.

00:00:55.350 --> 00:01:00.510
This learning algorithm is a Convolutional
Neural Network, which was trained on as little

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:06.060
as 3 to 5 minutes of footage per actor, and
was able to generalize its knowledge from

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:10.690
this training data to a variety of real-world
expressions and words.

00:01:10.690 --> 00:01:14.549
And if you think you've seen everything, you
should watch until the end of the video as

00:01:14.549 --> 00:01:17.770
it gets better than that because of two reasons.

00:01:17.770 --> 00:01:23.210
Reason number one, it not only takes audio
input, but we can also specify an emotional

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:32.530
state that the character should express when
uttering these words.

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:14.750
Number two, and this is the best part, we
can also combine this together with DeepMind's

00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:18.550
WaveNet, which synthesizes audio from our
text input.

00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:24.560
It basically synthesizes a believable human
voice and says whatever text we write down.

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:28.990
And then that sound clip can be used with
this technique to make a digital character

00:02:28.990 --> 00:02:30.690
say what we've written.

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:36.640
So we can go from text to speech with WaveNet,
and put the speech onto a virtual actor with this work.

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:06.560
This way, we get a whole pipeline that works
by learning and does everything for us in

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:08.569
the most convenient way.

00:03:08.569 --> 00:03:10.860
No actors needed for voiceovers.

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:13.180
No motion capture for animations.

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:15.379
This is truly incredible.

00:03:15.379 --> 00:03:19.200
And if you look at the left, side, you can
see that in their video, there is some Two

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:21.150
Minute Papers action going on.

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:22.630
How cool is that?

00:03:22.630 --> 00:03:26.659
Make sure to have a look at the paper to see
the three-way loss function the authors came

00:03:26.659 --> 00:03:31.150
up with to make sure that the results work
correctly for longer animations.

00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:36.049
And of course, in research, we have to prove
that our results are better than previous

00:03:36.049 --> 00:03:37.049
techniques.

00:03:37.049 --> 00:03:40.560
To accomplish this, there are plenty of comparisons
in the supplementary video.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:42.260
But we need more than that.

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:46.510
Since these results cannot be boiled down
to a mathematical theorem that we need to

00:03:46.510 --> 00:03:49.210
prove, we have to do it some other way.

00:03:49.210 --> 00:03:54.000
And the ultimate goal is that a human being
would judge these videos as being real with

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:57.800
a higher chance than one made with a previous
technique.

00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:01.890
This is the core idea behind the user study
carried out in the paper.

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:06.670
We bring in a bunch of people, present them
with a video of the old and new technique

00:04:06.670 --> 00:04:11.400
without knowing which is which, and ask them
which one they feel to be more natural.

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:16.549
And the result was not even close - the new
method is not only better overall, but I haven't

00:04:16.549 --> 00:04:21.739
found a single case, scenario or language
where it didn't come out ahead.

00:04:21.739 --> 00:04:24.000
And that's extremely rare in research.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:29.220
Typically, in a maturing field, new techniques
introduce a different kind of tradeoff, for

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:34.430
instance, less execution time but at the cost
of higher memory consumption is a classical

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:35.430
case.

00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:40.480
But here, it's just simply better in every
regard. Excellent.

